From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] localspl: monitors stay loaded, keep their MONITORINIT, and have the spooler's registry functions

What makers' language monitors expect of Windows' spooler:

- A monitor stays loaded once it is. Ours unloaded a monitor whenever
  nothing used it, and loaded and initialized it again for the next job.
  Kyocera's language monitor (KMPJL64) leaves a thread running in its DLL:
  when the DLL was unloaded the thread crashed the program, and every
  Kyocera job (and any job while that monitor was installed) failed. A
  monitor now keeps the spooler's own reference until DeleteMonitor.
- A monitor keeps the MONITORINIT it is given and its registry root while
  it is loaded. Ours was on the stack and the key was closed after
  InitializePrintMonitor2: Zebra's language monitor read its settings
  through it when a job started, crashed, and the job went past it.
- The spooler's registry functions for monitors (MONITORREG: CreateKey,
  OpenKey, QueryValue, SetValue and the rest) were stubs; they are the
  registry under the monitor's key now.

Test: test/netport-gate.sh (mutants SG_MUTANT_RESIDENTMON, MONINIT,
MONREG).
---
--- a/dlls/localspl/provider.c
+++ b/dlls/localspl/provider.c
@@ -207,6 +207,9 @@
     HANDLE          hmon;
     HMODULE         hdll;
     DWORD           refcount;
+    BOOL            resident;   /* the spooler's own reference */
+    MONITORINIT     init;       /* monitors keep the pointer they are given */
+    HKEY            root;       /* and the registry root in it */
 } monitor_t;
 
 typedef struct {
@@ -499,6 +502,7 @@
             pm->monitor.pfnShutdown(pm->hmon);
 
         FreeLibrary(pm->hdll);
+        if (pm->root) RegCloseKey(pm->root);
         free(pm->name);
         free(pm->dllname);
         free(pm);
@@ -686,38 +690,45 @@
